<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Opole and Racibórz</h1><p class="st-meta">1172 CE – 1281 CE · capital Racibórz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Piast duchy of Upper Silesia that emerged from the division of Silesia and held off the Mongols in 1241 before splitting into four.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the partition of the Polish realm, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a> was held by the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-the-exile">Władysław II the Exile</a>. In 1172 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-iv-tanglefoot">Mieszko IV Tanglefoot</a> received a separate share around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a>, the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a>. In 1202 he added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> district, creating a duchy independent of the Lower Silesian Duchy of Silesia.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Upper Silesian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piasts</a> ruled a largely Polish-speaking land of forests, farmland and river towns on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-river">Oder</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-i-of-opole">Kazimierz I</a> and his successors brought in German settlers and granted town charters under German law, founding or refounding places such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a>. During the Mongol invasion of 1241, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-the-fat">Mieszko II the Fat</a> fought the raiders near Racibórz and Opole.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Duke Władysław died in 1281, his four sons divided the duchy into the Duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a>. These Upper Silesian duchies drifted towards the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia</a> and accepted Bohemian overlordship in 1327.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-opole-and-raciborz.jpg" alt="Duchy of Opole and Racibórz" width="320" height="324"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silesia_1217-1230.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1172 CE – 1281 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Racibórz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mieszko IV Tanglefoot, Kazimierz I of Opole, Władysław of Opole</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Poland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Upper Silesia, Opole-Racibórz, Upper Silesian duchy, Duchy of Opole and Raciborz</dd></dl></aside></div>
